<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Without these, macOS does not prompt -- it denies outright. An app that asks for the
    microphone with no NSMicrophoneUsageDescription is refused by the system before the user ever
    sees a dialog, which is exactly why recording failed with no prompt and no obvious reason.

    These strings are what the prompt actually shows, so they say what the app does with the
    access and not merely that it wants it.
  -->
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Second Brain records meetings you start, so it can transcribe them on this Mac. Audio never leaves your machine.</string>

  <!--
    The Core Audio process tap in helper/recorder (Capture.swift) is what mixes system output into
    a recording, and Apple requires this key for it. Without it TCC refuses *silently*: the tap is
    created, no error comes back, and it delivers nothing -- so the recorder reported
    `systemAudio: true` for a session that captured the microphone alone. No prompt, no log line,
    nothing to tell anyone the other half of the meeting was never recorded.
  -->
  <key>NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Second Brain records the other side of a meeting -- what comes out of your speakers -- alongside your microphone, so the transcript has both halves. Audio never leaves your machine.</string>

  <key>NSCalendarsUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Second Brain reads your calendar to show your meetings and offer to record them.</string>

  <!-- macOS 14 and newer ask for calendar access through this key instead of the one above. -->
  <key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Second Brain reads your calendar to show your meetings and offer to record them.</string>

  <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Second Brain asks your browser for the page you are on, so activity tracking can tell one tab from another.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
